usb: force a fresh host-mode probe in usb_init()

On boards with a dual-role ChipIdea/ci_udc controller shared between
USB host and a USB gadget function (e.g. a console running over
usbacm, or CONFIG_USB_GADGET_DOWNLOAD's DFU), the gadget side claims
the controller during boot via usb_setup_ehci_gadget(): it
device_remove()s the UCLASS_USB device, sets plat->init_type =
USB_INIT_DEVICE, and device_probe()s it again in that role.

usb_remove_ehci_gadget() (called when that gadget function is done)
only device_remove()s the device again -- it never restores
init_type. So plat->init_type stays USB_INIT_DEVICE for that device
for the rest of the boot. usb_init() (the "usb start" command) never
touches init_type at all, and device_probe() is a no-op on a device
that isn't currently removed/inactive, so a device-mode probe from
earlier in boot is never replaced by a host probe: "usb start"
registers u-boot's own root hub fine (that part doesn't depend on the
controller's mode) but scans a controller still configured as a
gadget, and any real transaction (e.g. the async schedule, STS_ASS)
never comes up.

"usb start" always means host. Force plat->init_type = USB_INIT_HOST
for every USB bus device before probing it, and if the device is
already active in some other mode, device_remove() it first so the
new init_type actually takes effect on a fresh probe.

--- a/drivers/usb/host/usb-uclass.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/usb-uclass.c
@@ -305,10 +305,27 @@ int usb_init(void)
 	uc_priv = uclass_get_priv(uc);

 	uclass_foreach_dev(bus, uc) {
+		struct usb_plat *plat = dev_get_plat(bus);
+
 		/* init low_level USB */
 		printf("Bus %s: ", bus->name);

 		/*
+		 * A dual-role controller (e.g. ChipIdea/ci_udc) may already
+		 * be actively probed as a USB gadget via
+		 * usb_setup_ehci_gadget() for a console or DFU function.
+		 * device_probe() below is a no-op on an already-active
+		 * device, so without forcing a fresh probe here a device-mode
+		 * probe from earlier boot would never be replaced by a host
+		 * probe -- "usb start" would silently scan a controller still
+		 * configured as a gadget. "usb start" always means host, so
+		 * force both the type and a fresh probe here.
+		 */
+		if (device_active(bus) && plat->init_type != USB_INIT_HOST)
+			device_remove(bus, DM_REMOVE_NORMAL);
+		plat->init_type = USB_INIT_HOST;
+
+		/*
 		 * For Sandbox, we need scan the device tree each time when we
 		 * start the USB stack, in order to re-create the emulated USB
 		 * devices and bind drivers for them before we actually do the
